      "side": 1,
      "message": "nit: Maybe just \"ordered\"? According to RFC 8949, \"canonical\" is mostly associated with the ordering in RFC 7049\n\n\u003e Although [RFC7049] used the term \"Canonical CBOR\" for its form of requirements on deterministic encoding, this document avoids this term because \"canonicalization\" is often associated with specific uses of deterministic encoding only",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 31,
        "startChar": 7,
        "endLine": 31,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "46a7f926826535cf985385b6604c18cf4ff60c68",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5a243640_c52aebf0",
        "filename": "security/rkp/aidl/android/hardware/security/keymint/DeviceInfo.aidl",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 31,
      "author": {
        "id": 1139038
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-03-08T13:55:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If the fields don\u0027t all need to be deterministically encoded and it\u0027s just the order that\u0027s important then \"ordered\" should be used. Otherwise, RFC 8949 uses the catchy term \"length-first core deterministic encoding requirements\" to describe this specific flavour of canonical form. https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8949#section-4.2.3-1",
      "parentUuid": "2ba135b1_7fe58228",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 31,
        "startChar": 7,
        "endLine": 31,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "46a7f926826535cf985385b6604c18cf4ff60c68",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
